import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Backend","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"BACKEND.md","filePath":"BACKEND.md"}'),d={name:"BACKEND.md"};function o(r,e,i,h,c,l){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="backend" tabindex="-1">Backend <a class="header-anchor" href="#backend" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backend&quot;">​</a></h1><p>llamatron&#39;s backend, in <code>src/backend/</code>, plays the built-in synths and FluidSynth, and sends every MIDI event on time. This page explains how it works and how to change it, for a reader who knows some programming but perhaps no Rust. <a href="./BUILDING_THE_BACKEND">Building llamatron</a> says how to build it.</p><h2 id="why-it-stands-apart" tabindex="-1">Why It Stands Apart <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-it-stands-apart" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why It Stands Apart&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A synthesizer must hand the sound card a new chunk of sound every few milliseconds, on time, every time; a late chunk is heard as a click. Rust compiles to native code with no pauses to tidy memory, so one voice of a synth costs a few microseconds a chunk. The backend keeps to its own threads, apart from the tracker&#39;s drawing, so that a page drawn never makes a note late, and it is a library of its own, so that another program can drive it too.</p><h2 id="how-it-runs" tabindex="-1">How It Runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How It Runs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>llamatron links the backend as a library and runs it on threads of its own: the sender while a song or a preview plays, and the renderer for the WAV export&#39;s built-in tracks, each handed its messages through a channel. The same code also builds as a program, <code>llamatron-backend</code>, which another program can start and speak the same messages to through its standard input and output:</p><ul><li><strong><code>send</code></strong>: the real-time sender, which sends every event on time through the MIDI ports, the built-in synths and FluidSynth (<code>sender/</code>, <code>daemon/sending.rs</code>).</li><li><strong><code>play</code></strong>: the built-in synths alone.</li><li><strong><code>render</code></strong>: no sound card; it plays the WAV export&#39;s built-in tracks into a file, as fast as it can.</li><li><strong><code>ports</code></strong>: lists the computer&#39;s MIDI destinations, a line each, and ends.</li></ul><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Message</th><th>What it carries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>to the engine</td><td><code>instrument</code></td><td>An instrument&#39;s number and its sound, every setting of every part.</td></tr><tr><td>to the engine</td><td><code>notes</code></td><td>Notes, each a time, a kind (start, end, every note ending, a clip starting, or taking notes back), an instrument, a pitch and a velocity.</td></tr><tr><td>to the engine</td><td><code>silence</code></td><td>Stop every sound at once, as Ctrl+T does.</td></tr><tr><td>to the engine</td><td><code>close</code></td><td>End.</td></tr><tr><td>from the engine</td><td><code>ready</code></td><td>The sample rate and how long every note is held back.</td></tr><tr><td>from the engine</td><td><code>failed</code></td><td>Why the engine could not start.</td></tr><tr><td>from the engine</td><td><code>load</code></td><td>Twice a second: how busy the engine is, its voices, and any dropouts.</td></tr><tr><td>from the engine</td><td><code>statistics</code></td><td>As it closes: its timing over the whole run.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each message is a MessagePack array, a compact binary form of a list, with its length written before it in four bytes, so that the reader always knows where one message ends (<code>src/backend/src/protocol/</code>).</p><p>A note&#39;s time is a moment on the computer&#39;s own steady clock, which the tracker and the engine both read (<code>audio/clock.rs</code>), so that they agree on when a note falls without converting anything.</p><h2 id="the-sender" tabindex="-1">The Sender <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sender" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Sender&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The sender (<code>sender/</code>) sends each event at its moment. Each event arrives as a small record: its seconds on the timeline, its play&#39;s tag (a scene and a track), its port, and its MIDI bytes. On macOS each event is handed to its port a tenth of a second ahead, stamped with its moment: CoreMIDI delivers a MIDI port&#39;s event then, and the built-in synths and FluidSynth place theirs on the exact sample. What is handed can be taken back: stopping a clip, stopping everything, or an edit heard at once flushes the ports holding what is dropped, hands back what they keep, and undoes what handing the dropped events did to the notes counted as sounding, so that a stop silences exactly what has sounded. Elsewhere each event is sent as its moment comes, the sender&#39;s thread sleeping and then spinning up to it.</p><h2 id="threads" tabindex="-1">Threads <a class="header-anchor" href="#threads" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Threads&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Inside the engine, three threads share the work, so that the one that makes the sound never waits.</p><ul><li><strong>The reader</strong> reads the tracker&#39;s messages. It compiles each instrument, building its wavetables, and renders each drum hit the first time it is needed, then hands both to the audio thread.</li><li><strong>The audio thread</strong> is the sound card&#39;s own. Each time the card asks for a chunk, it takes what the reader handed over, places each note on its exact sample, plays every voice and mixes the result. It never allocates memory, never waits on a lock and never touches a file.</li><li><strong>The housekeeper</strong> frees what the audio thread has finished with, such as an instrument that has been replaced, so that the audio thread never frees memory either.</li></ul><p>The reader and the audio thread pass things through lock-free queues (<code>rtrb</code>), which one thread fills and the other empties without either ever waiting for the other.</p><h2 id="placing-a-note-on-its-sample" tabindex="-1">Placing a Note on Its Sample <a class="header-anchor" href="#placing-a-note-on-its-sample" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Placing a Note on Its Sample&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The sound card says, with each chunk, when the chunk&#39;s first sample will reach the speakers. Every note is held back by the same delay, the card&#39;s own latency and one chunk, so that a note sent on time is never late. The audio thread then counts how many samples into the chunk the note&#39;s moment falls, and the note starts on exactly that sample, whatever the chunk&#39;s size. MIDI sent to other devices is held back by the same delay, so the two land together.</p><h2 id="the-code-s-folders" tabindex="-1">The Code&#39;s Folders <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-code-s-folders" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Code&#39;s Folders&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Folder</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>protocol/</code></td><td>The messages, and reading the tracker&#39;s values with a default for anything left out.</td></tr><tr><td><code>daemon/</code></td><td>The two ways of running, playing and rendering, and the drum hits rendered as notes arrive.</td></tr><tr><td><code>audio/</code></td><td>The sound card&#39;s stream and its callback, the clock, and WAV files.</td></tr><tr><td><code>engine/</code></td><td>Notes and MIDI scheduled at exact samples, the mix, and the safety guard.</td></tr><tr><td><code>voice/</code></td><td>The parts of a built-in instrument&#39;s voice, and the patch that plays them together.</td></tr><tr><td><code>drums/</code></td><td>MechaDrum&#39;s recipes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>fluid/</code></td><td>FluidSynth, loaded at run time.</td></tr><tr><td><code>dsp/</code></td><td>Filters&#39; building blocks and a seeded random source, which every part shares.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-voice" tabindex="-1">The Voice <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-voice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Voice&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every built-in instrument but the drum machine is a voice of swappable parts, the same parts the Instrument page shows.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>File</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oscillators</td><td><code>oscillators/</code> (a file each), <code>oscillators/tables.rs</code></td><td>The raw sound: Basic&#39;s band-limited waveforms, the SID-style chip, the Pocket chip, the acid bass&#39;s saw or square.</td></tr><tr><td>Env1, Env2, Env3</td><td><code>envelopes/</code> (a file each)</td><td>Env1 shapes the loudness, Env2 the filter&#39;s sweep, Env3 whatever the Mod Matrix sends it to: ADSR, AR, ASR, the SID chip&#39;s ADSR, the vactrol, the acid gate and the chip&#39;s sixteen steps.</td></tr><tr><td>LFO1 to LFO4</td><td><code>modulation/lfos.rs</code></td><td>Slow movement for the mod matrix.</td></tr><tr><td>Filter</td><td><code>filters/mod.rs</code></td><td>The ladder, diode, OTA, multimode and Sallen-Key filters, or the low-pass gate.</td></tr><tr><td>Matrix</td><td><code>modulation/matrix.rs</code></td><td>Six routes from a source, such as an LFO or the velocity, to a destination, such as the cutoff.</td></tr><tr><td>FX1, FX2</td><td><code>effects/</code></td><td>Bitcrush, Drive, Bax and MultiTap, a file each.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>instrument/patch.rs</code> puts the parts together into an instrument and plays its notes one sample at a time. The filters are zero-delay-feedback models: each sample, the filter&#39;s loop is solved exactly, so that the cutoff can sweep as fast as an envelope moves it without stepping or blowing up, and the ladder at full resonance rings on by itself as the hardware does.</p><p>The drum machine, MechaDrum (<code>drums/</code>), renders each hit whole when its note first arrives, from its five knobs, and replays it. The acoustic tom is a physical model of a struck drum head&#39;s modes, and the Karplus drum a plucked string turned drum.</p><h2 id="fluidsynth" tabindex="-1">FluidSynth <a class="header-anchor" href="#fluidsynth" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FluidSynth&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Where FluidSynth is installed, the engine plays it too, in the same audio callback (<code>fluid/</code>). The tracker gives it the path of FluidSynth&#39;s library, found beside the installed <code>fluidsynth</code> program, and the SoundFont; the engine loads the library at run time, so building the engine never needs FluidSynth. The MIDI for FluidSynth&#39;s instruments arrives timed like every note, and the engine renders FluidSynth up to each message&#39;s sample before playing the message, so that each lands within FluidSynth&#39;s own block of 64 samples. The mix is stereo: the built-in instruments in the middle, FluidSynth&#39;s sounds where General MIDI pans them, each channel through its own safety guard.</p><h2 id="safety" tabindex="-1">Safety <a class="header-anchor" href="#safety" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Safety&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>engine/safety.rs</code> keeps every protection of the built-in synths, which no setting can turn off. An instrument that produces a number that is not finite, or a level far past anything a patch makes, is silenced and reset. The whole mix then passes a filter that removes what is too low or too high to hear, a limiter that holds it under full scale, a slow guard against sound staying very loud, a final soft clip, and a latch that fades everything out after repeated faults until Ctrl+T.</p><h2 id="never-left-behind" tabindex="-1">Never Left Behind <a class="header-anchor" href="#never-left-behind" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Never Left Behind&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The engine exits when its input closes, which happens when the tracker closes it or the tracker itself ends, however it ends. It also watches its parent process and exits if that disappears, and on Linux the system tells it at once. The tracker, for its part, asks it to close, waits two seconds and then ends it. A problem inside the audio thread plays silence and is counted, never stopping the sound card&#39;s stream, and a problem anywhere else is written to the log in <code>~/.llamatron/</code> and told to the tracker.</p><h2 id="rendering-a-wav-file" tabindex="-1">Rendering a WAV File <a class="header-anchor" href="#rendering-a-wav-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rendering a WAV File&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The WAV export plays the built-in tracks through the same engine, offline: the tracker starts it with <code>llamatron-backend render</code>, sends the instruments and every note timed from the song&#39;s start, and the engine plays them through the same voices and the same safety guard into a file, as fast as it can.</p><h2 id="changing-the-backend" tabindex="-1">Changing the Backend <a class="header-anchor" href="#changing-the-backend" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changing the Backend&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>.claude/rules/RUST.md</code> holds the rules the code follows. In short:</p><ul><li><code>cargo fmt</code> lays out the code, and <code>cargo clippy</code> with every warning an error checks it; <code>internal_tools/check_before_commit.sh</code> runs both, and <code>cargo test</code>, whenever a file under <code>src/</code> changes.</li><li>Nothing in the audio thread may allocate, lock or block.</li><li><code>unsafe</code> is used only where the engine calls the operating system, each with a comment saying why it is sound.</li><li>Each module&#39;s tests sit at its end, in <code>mod tests</code>, and run with <code>cargo test --release --manifest-path src/Cargo.toml</code>.</li></ul>',36)])])}const p=t(d,[["render",o]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
